ps and then included  Afghanistan so basically if you weren't  our ally that means you are a terrorist  but in fact the situation on the ground  is a lot more complicated there's a lot  more gray area the al Qaeda had fled the  country in 2002 and the Taliban from the  rank and file all the way to the senior  leadership pretty much had stopped  fighting so we were left for the  situation where there's no enemy to  fight but we had thousands of soldiers  there ready  what ended up happening those we allied  with warlords who had their own agendas  so for example if you had a piece of  land and I was a warlord and I wanted  that land I could tell the US military  that you're a member of the Taliban and  since I was the only intelligence they  had they would arrest you  interesting so so that led to a lot of  corruption I think we're still only  beginning to recognize what we are role  of our complicity in this process in the  early years is one of the things I hope  to do with my book unfortunately a lot  of this history has been lost because  this is stuff that's happened deep in  the countryside where reporters usually  don't go where Afghans only should have  a chance to speak to the international  media they don't write books or write  these people articles to describe their  experiences so with the Karzai regime  you you say you don't think that they  would last a day without the American  support that they currently have can you  talk a bit about this because they're  getting so much money I mean where is it  going  well actually yes seventy percent of the  Afghan budget comes from foreign aid  they're not able to do some basic things  like even raise taxes or anything else  so they're entirely reliant on  international aid to survive and if we  stopped paying for the government  tomorrow it would collapse the army  would collapse the police would collapse  and most of the money that's going into  Afghanistan actually bypasses the  government if you look at the 